;; A control of what the test tool prints (see ../../tools/flsemantics.ml,
;; the head of `main`): no escape sequence of a terminal, whether or not
;; the tool runs Simplify. The fault that it is for is discrepancy N3-2 of
;; the list in ../../../rocq/DECISIONS.md.
;;
;; The compiler colours the names that it prints unless that has been
;; decided otherwise before the first of them is printed, and it is decided
;; once. The tool runs Simplify through `Flambda2.flambda_to_flambda`, which
;; decides it, against colours where the error output is not a terminal.
;; With the option -after the tool reads the term after from a text and
;; does not run Simplify: before the tool decided it itself, at the head of
;; `main`, a name in one of its lines came out then between the escape
;; sequences that colour it. So did, with or without that option, a kind in
;; a fatal error of the compiler that is raised as the tool reads a text,
;; which is before any run of Simplify; no rule here is of that.
;;
;; name_in_a_refusal: a pair under -after whose line of the check of regions
;; names a variable (the two texts say why). name_in_a_refusal.expected is
;; what the tool prints of it, and has no escape byte: without the call at
;; the head of `main` the tool prints that line with three, and the rule
;; below fails. `dune promote` updates the file; a file promoted with an
;; escape byte in it is the fault that this control is for. The number in
;; the name of the variable is the compiler's own for it, and it moves when
;; the tool makes another number of variables before it reads the texts:
;; promote then, and look that the file has no escape byte.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  name_in_a_refusal.output
  (run
   ../../tools/flsemantics.exe
   -after
   %{dep:name_in_a_refusal_after.fl}
   %{dep:name_in_a_refusal.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff name_in_a_refusal.expected name_in_a_refusal.output)))
